// HallCondition — the feasibility test for a program point whose live values have DIFFERENT
// effective pools (design: ARCHITECTURE.md's register-allocator section, "Known limits" #0).
//
// WHAT IT IS FOR. `maxPressure <= pool` is NECESSARY but not SUFFICIENT, because a forbidden set
// SHRINKS a value's pool: a value live across a `callDirect` is forbidden all 9 caller-saved
// registers, so its only home is one of the 5 CALLEE-SAVED; a value live across an `idiv` cannot be
// RAX/RDX; a value live across an argument pre-move cannot be that argument register. Two values at
// the same point can therefore be shopping in two different, differently sized register sets, and
// counting them against one number cannot decide whether they fit.
//
// THE CASE THE OLD TEST COULD NOT SEE. The splitter used to test each op two ways: `effective >
// |pool|` (the full pool) and `raw > popcount(pool ∖ op.implicitDefs)` (the op's own reduced pool).
// The second only ever fires AT A CLOBBER OP, so it is blind to exactly this:
//
//     several values are each confined to the callee-saved subset — but by DIFFERENT calls — and
//     are simultaneously live at a point that is NOT ITSELF A CALL.
//
// At that point the pool is nominally the full 14, so the peak-finder saw nothing; no call had more
// than five values live across it, so the reduced-pool test saw nothing either. The splitter
// relieved nothing and the COLORER then ran out of registers ("no free register", every callee-saved
// one taken). Six values, each used after a call in its own arm of an `if`/`else if` chain, are
// enough: each is live across only ITS OWN call (liveness is path-sensitive — a value used only in
// arm 3 is dead in arm 1), yet all six are live together at the chain's first `cmp`.
//
// THE MODEL. At a program point, give each live value `v` its ALLOWED set `A(v) = pool ∖
// forbidden(v)`. Colouring the point is a system of distinct representatives — a bipartite matching
// between values and registers — and by HALL'S THEOREM it is possible iff no register set `U` has
// more values confined to it than it has registers:
//
//     for every U ⊆ pool:   |{ v : A(v) ⊆ U }|  <=  |U|
//
// This SUBSUMES both old tests. `U = pool` is the full-pool test (every value is confined to the
// pool, so it is plain pigeonhole). `U = callee-saved` at a call is the reduced-pool test (every
// value live across the call is confined to it). And it additionally sees the case above, whose
// witness `U` is the callee-saved set at a point that is not a call.
//
// HOW IT IS COMPUTED, AND WHY IN TWO STAGES. Enumerating every `U` is exponential, so:
//
//  1. A CARDINALITY SCREEN, O(live + 16), runs at every op (`RegPoolCensus.screenViolates`): bucket
//     the live values by `|A(v)|` and prefix-sum, so `C(p) = #{v : |A(v)| <= p}`; report a possible
//     violation if `C(p) > p` for some `p`. This NEVER MISSES a real one — every value of a
//     violating tight set has `|A(v)| <= |U|`, so a violation at `U` always trips the screen at
//     `p = |U|`. It is maintained INCREMENTALLY across the backward sweep, exactly as the live count
//     is, and skipped outright when no live value is constrained (every op of a call-free function).
//
//  2. An EXACT CONFIRMATION, run only where the screen fires (`hallVerdictAt`): a maximum bipartite
//     matching. Feasible iff it saturates the live values; the deficit is `|live| - |matching|`
//     (the König–Ore deficiency), and the tight set / witness `U` falls out of the same search.
//
// The screen alone would NOT be exact, and the difference matters: the forbidden sets are NOT
// laminar. `ParamCrossForbid` forbids each parameter its SIBLINGS' incoming registers,
// so with three parameters the masks are `{rdx,rax}`, `{rcx,rax}`, `{rcx,rdx}` — same size,
// pairwise INCOMPARABLE, and jointly colourable. A cardinality-only test can call such a group an
// overflow when it fits, and the spill it would then emit is one the program did not need. The
// confirmation is what keeps this from adding pressure relief to code that was already fine — the
// same discipline that makes a false E5001 the worst bug this compiler can have.
//
// COST. The confirmation runs only where the screen fired and the point does NOT overflow the full
// pool — so `|live| <= |pool| <= RegisterFileSize` there, and the whole search is bounded by 16
// values × 16 registers. Everything else on the per-op path is the O(16) prefix scan, behind a
// zero-constrained-values gate.
//
// THAT BOUND IS ON ONE CALL, AND IT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT HOW OFTEN THE CALL HAPPENS — which is where
// the cost actually was. Confinement is a property of a value's WHOLE LIVE RANGE, not of the clobber
// op: N accumulators live across a call in a loop are confined to the 5 callee-saved registers at
// EVERY op of that loop. So for N = 8 the screen's O(1) early-out (`constrained <= smallestPool`, i.e.
// `8 <= 5`) does NOT fire, the prefix scan reports `C(5) = 8 > 5`, and the exact matching runs at
// every op of the loop — on every one of the splitter's iterations. The search being small does not
// make it free when it is entered thousands of times, and the matching used to HEAP-ALLOCATE its five
// working columns on every entry (two of them PER VALUE, inside `augmentValue`). `HallScratch` is
// those five buffers, allocated once per function and reused — the discipline ARCHITECTURE.md states
// for the whole allocator ("scratch buffers: allocated once, reused across functions") and that this
// file was the last place not to follow.

// A value's position in ONE PROGRAM POINT's live-value list — the "values" side of the bipartite
// matching below, and the key its two inverse maps are stated in. It is NOT a ValueId (the matching
// renumbers the point's live values 0..live.count()) and NOT a CfgIndex, which is what it used to be
// typed as: a block index and a live-set position are unrelated spaces that happened to share a
// representation. `live.count()` is its "no value" sentinel.
typealias LiveIndex = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias LiveIndexColumn = Array with LiveIndex

// The registers `valueId` may actually be coloured into: the registers of ITS OWN CLASS that the pool
// offers, minus every register an op wrote while the value was live across it (`forbidden` — a call's
// 9 caller-saved, an `idiv`'s RAX/RDX, an argument pre-move's target). This is the ONE definition of a
// value's effective pool; the colorer blocks on the same mask (`chooseRegister`), so the two cannot
// disagree about what fits.
//
// ⚠ THE CLASS FILTER IS PART OF THE DEFINITION, NOT A REFINEMENT OF IT. Without it an f64's allowed
// set is computed from the GPR pool it can never touch, and every downstream question inherits the
// error — `valueConfinedTo` most damagingly (see there).
export function allowedRegistersOf(liveness LivenessResult, valueId ValueId, pool RegMask) returns RegMask
	return poolOfClass(pool, class: liveness.classOf(valueId)) and not liveness.forbiddenOf(valueId)
end 'allowedRegistersOf'

// Is `valueId` confined to `witness` — is EVERY register it could take inside that set? This is
// membership of the tight set an overflow is about, and it is what makes a victim choice
// meaningful: spilling a value that could have taken a register OUTSIDE the witness frees nothing
// the confined values were able to use. For a FULL-pool peak the witness IS the class's whole pool, so
// every value of that class is confined to it and the test is a no-op — which is why the E5001 path is
// untouched by it.
//
// ⚠⚠ THE EMPTY ALLOWED SET IS THE ONE HOLE IN THE SUBSET TEST, AND IT IS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER.
// `witness` is a bag of bits that does not know which file it names, so the subset test spans both —
// and ∅ IS A SUBSET OF EVERY SET. An f64 live across a call is forbidden every XMM, so its allowed set
// is EMPTY, so it would test CONFINED to a callee-saved GPR witness produced by an int-only overflow.
// `chooseVictim` would then spill a FLOAT to relieve a GPR peak, freeing an XMM no int can use: the
// peak survives, the potential Φ does not fall, and the driver re-picks forever.
//
// Closing it needs no witness class, because a class-filtered `allowedRegistersOf` (see above) makes
// every NON-EMPTY allowed set a subset of its own file — and two files are disjoint, so such a set can
// never be confined to a foreign witness. ∅ is the only mask that is confined to a witness it shares
// no file with, and a value with no legal register at all is not "confined to" anything: it is
// unrelievable in place, which is a question about the value and not about this witness.
export function valueConfinedTo(liveness LivenessResult, valueId ValueId, pool RegMask, witness RegMask) returns bool
	let allowed = allowedRegistersOf(liveness, valueId: valueId, pool: pool)
	if allowed == 0 'noLegalRegisterAtAll'
		return false
	end 'noLegalRegisterAtAll'
	return (allowed and not witness) == 0
end 'valueConfinedTo'

// Per-value EFFECTIVE POOL SIZE — `popcount(pool ∖ forbidden(v))` — plus how many values in the
// function are constrained at all.
//
// `sizes` is one dense column, so maintaining the per-op census costs an array read instead of a
// popcount.
//
// It is MAINTAINED, not rebuilt. A value's effective pool is a function of its `forbidden` mask, and a
// split changes that mask for exactly two kinds of value — the victim, whose live range it cut, and the
// fresh ids it minted. Re-deriving the whole column per split was Θ(splits × values), which is the
// quadratic the incremental splitter exists to remove; `refresh` re-derives ONE value, and keeps the
// constrained tally exact as it goes.
export type EffectivePools
	export var sizes as RegCountColumn
	// How many values are CONSTRAINED (an effective pool strictly smaller than THEIR OWN CLASS's full
	// one). Zero in a call-free, idiv-free function, which is what lets its pressure sweeps skip the
	// census entirely.
	var constrained as ColumnLength

	export static function create() returns EffectivePools
		return Self{sizes: RegCountColumn.create(), constrained: 0}
	end 'create'

	// Fill the whole column — ONCE per function, before its first split. Every entry in
	// [0, valueCount) is written, so a longer previous life cannot leak a stale size (see
	// `Array.refill`, which is this loop generalised; this one stays hand-written because it also
	// counts `constrained` as it goes).
	export function fillAll(liveness LivenessResult, pool RegMask)
		self.sizes.resize(liveness.valueCount)
		self.constrained = 0

		for v in 0 upto liveness.valueCount 'eachValue'
			let size = regMaskPopcount(allowedRegistersOf(liveness, valueId: v, pool: pool))
			try self.sizes.set(v, value: size) otherwise panic("EffectivePools.fillAll: sizes.set OOB for value {v}")

			if size < classPoolSizeOf(pool, class: liveness.classOf(v)) 'constrainedValue'
				self.constrained = self.constrained + 1
			end 'constrainedValue'
		end 'eachValue'
	end 'fillAll'

	// Extend to the ids a split MINTED. A fresh id forbids nothing until the dirty-region sweep says
	// otherwise, so it arrives with the full pool OF ITS CLASS and counts as unconstrained; `refresh`
	// corrects both the moment its mask is known. `class` is the victim's — see
	// `LivenessResult.growValueSpace`, which is where a fresh id's class comes from and why.
	export function growTo(valueCount ValueId, pool RegMask, class RegClass)
		self.sizes.growFilled(valueCount, value: classPoolSizeOf(pool, class: class))
	end 'growTo'

	// Re-derive ONE value's effective pool from its (just rebuilt) forbidden mask, keeping the
	// constrained tally exact. A value's CLASS needs no maintenance here and never will: it is
	// intrinsic and permanent, so a split — which only ever cuts a live range and rebuilds a forbidden
	// mask — cannot move a value between files. That is the same property that keeps the class out of
	// `forbidden` entirely.
	export function refresh(liveness LivenessResult, valueId ValueId, pool RegMask)
		let fullPoolSize = classPoolSizeOf(pool, class: liveness.classOf(valueId))
		let previous = try self.sizes.get(valueId) otherwise panic("EffectivePools.refresh: sizes.get OOB for value {valueId}")
		let size = regMaskPopcount(allowedRegistersOf(liveness, valueId: valueId, pool: pool))
		try self.sizes.set(valueId, value: size) otherwise panic("EffectivePools.refresh: sizes.set OOB for value {valueId}")

		if (previous < fullPoolSize) == (size < fullPoolSize) 'sameConstraintClass'
			return
		end 'sameConstraintClass'

		if size < fullPoolSize 'becameConstrained'
			self.constrained = self.constrained + 1
		end 'becameConstrained' else 'becameFree'
			self.constrained = self.constrained - 1
		end 'becameFree'
	end 'refresh'

	// Is ANY value in the function constrained at all? With no forbidden mask anywhere, every value's
	// allowed set IS the pool: no register subset can be tight, Hall's condition cannot fail against a
	// proper subset, and the only overflow possible is the full-pool one the caller already tests on its
	// own. Maintaining a distribution that provably cannot violate anything is pure cost — and it is not
	// a rare case: a compiler's leaf functions are mostly call-free, and the census is stepped over
	// EVERY OPERAND OF EVERY OP.
	export function anyConstrained() returns bool
		return self.constrained > 0
	end 'anyConstrained'
end 'EffectivePools'

// ==========================================================================
// Stage 1 — the cardinality screen (per op, O(live + 16))
// ==========================================================================

// The live set's census BY EFFECTIVE POOL SIZE: how many live values have exactly `k` registers
// available to them. Hall's condition needs the whole DISTRIBUTION, not just the total, which is
// precisely what a single `liveCount` cannot carry.
//
// Maintained INCREMENTALLY across the backward sweep (`stepCensusBackwardOverOp`), for the same
// reason `liveCount` is: recomputing it would be a walk of the live set at every op — O(ops × live)
// per sweep, per splitter iteration — which is the quadratic the sparse representation exists to
// remove.
export type RegPoolCensus
	// effective-pool size (0 .. RegisterFileSize) -> how many live values have it.
	var byPoolSize as RegCountColumn
	// Which buckets of `byPoolSize` are NON-EMPTY, as a bitmask. Exists so the SMALLEST effective
	// pool among the live values is a trailing-zero count instead of a scan for the first non-empty
	// bucket — see `screenViolates`, which needs it on every op.
	var occupied as MachineWord
	// How many live values are CONSTRAINED (effective pool strictly smaller than the full pool).
	// Zero at every op of a call-free, idiv-free function.
	var constrained as RegCount

	export static function create() returns RegPoolCensus
		return Self{byPoolSize: zeroColumn(RegisterFileSize + 1), occupied: 0, constrained: 0}
	end 'create'

	export function reset()
		for size in 0 upto RegisterFileSize + 1 'eachSize'
			try self.byPoolSize.set(size, value: 0) otherwise panic("RegPoolCensus.reset: byPoolSize.set OOB at {size}")
		end 'eachSize'
		self.occupied = 0
		self.constrained = 0
	end 'reset'

	export function add(poolSize RegCount, fullPoolSize RegCount)
		let current = try self.byPoolSize.get(poolSize) otherwise panic("RegPoolCensus.add: pool size {poolSize} out of range")
		try self.byPoolSize.set(poolSize, value: current + 1) otherwise panic("RegPoolCensus.add: byPoolSize.set OOB")

		if current == 0 'bucketBecameOccupied'
			self.occupied = self.occupied or (1 shl poolSize)
		end 'bucketBecameOccupied'

		if poolSize < fullPoolSize 'constrainedValue'
			self.constrained = self.constrained + 1
		end 'constrainedValue'
	end 'add'

	export function remove(poolSize RegCount, fullPoolSize RegCount)
		let current = try self.byPoolSize.get(poolSize) otherwise panic("RegPoolCensus.remove: pool size {poolSize} out of range")
		if current == 0 'underflow'
			panic("RegPoolCensus.remove: no live value has an effective pool of {poolSize} — the census has drifted from the live row it mirrors")
		end 'underflow'
		try self.byPoolSize.set(poolSize, value: current - 1) otherwise panic("RegPoolCensus.remove: byPoolSize.set OOB")

		if current == 1 'bucketBecameEmpty'
			self.occupied = self.occupied and not (1 shl poolSize)
		end 'bucketBecameEmpty'

		if poolSize < fullPoolSize 'constrainedValue'
			self.constrained = self.constrained - 1
		end 'constrainedValue'
	end 'remove'

	// Does the cardinality screen see a possible Hall violation against a PROPER SUBSET of the pool?
	//
	// `C(p) = #{ live v : |A(v)| <= p }`, and `C(p) > p` for some `p < fullPoolSize` means more values
	// are confined to `p`-or-fewer registers than `p` registers exist. That is NECESSARY for a real
	// violation (every value of a tight set `U` has `|A(v)| <= |U|`, so the violation at `U` shows up
	// here at `p = |U|`) but not sufficient — see the file header on why the masks are not laminar —
	// so a `true` here is a call for the exact confirmation, never a verdict.
	//
	// `p == fullPoolSize` is deliberately NOT scanned: that is the FULL-pool overflow, whose demand
	// is `effective` (live values PLUS the dead-phi reservations PLUS the reuse-copy transient) and
	// not the live count alone. The caller tests it separately and it always outranks this one.
	export function screenViolates(fullPoolSize RegCount) returns bool
		if self.constrained == 0 'nothingConfined'
			return false
		end 'nothingConfined'

		// The smallest effective pool among the live values. `constrained > 0` implies at least one
		// live value, so `occupied` is non-zero and the trailing-zero count is defined.
		let smallestPool = trailingZeroCount(self.occupied)

		// O(1) EXACT EARLY-OUT — this is what keeps the screen off the per-op critical path.
		//
		// A violation needs `C(p) > p` for some `p < fullPoolSize`. Two facts bound `C`:
		//   * `C(p) = 0` for every `p < smallestPool` — no live value has a pool that small.
		//   * `C(p) <= constrained` for every `p < fullPoolSize` — a value counted by `C(p)` has
		//     `|A(v)| <= p < fullPoolSize`, so it is by definition constrained.
		// So a violating `p` must satisfy `constrained > p >= smallestPool`. If `constrained` does not
		// exceed `smallestPool`, no such `p` exists and the prefix scan cannot find one.
		//
		// This is the overwhelmingly common shape at an op inside a call-heavy function: a couple of
		// values live across a call (constrained = 2, smallestPool = 5, the callee-saved count), which
		// is nowhere near an overflow. Without it, every op of every function containing a call pays a
		// full 14-iteration prefix scan to be told the same thing.
		if self.constrained <= smallestPool 'cannotViolate'
			return false
		end 'cannotViolate'

		// Start at `smallestPool`: `C(p)` is zero below it, so those iterations can only compare 0 > p.
		var confinedSoFar = 0 as RegCount
		for p in smallestPool upto fullPoolSize 'prefixSum'
			confinedSoFar = confinedSoFar + (try self.byPoolSize.get(p) otherwise panic("RegPoolCensus.screenViolates: byPoolSize.get OOB at {p}"))
			if confinedSoFar > p 'violates'
				return true
			end 'violates'
		end 'prefixSum'
		return false
	end 'screenViolates'
end 'RegPoolCensus'

// Step the live ROW and its CENSUS backward over one op together, returning the new live count.
//
// The census delta is OBSERVED, never re-derived. A backward step flips only the op's OWN virtual
// operands, so their live bits are recorded BEFORE `stepRowBackwardOverOperands` and compared AFTER.
// Re-deriving which bits flip would duplicate that transfer function in a second place, and the two
// silently drifting apart is exactly the class of pressure bug this file exists to end.
//
// `ids`/`wasLive` are caller-owned scratch, reused across every op so nothing is allocated in the
// sweep. The snapshot DEDUPES: an op may name one value twice (`x + x`, or a reuse def whose input
// is also its use), and counting a single flip twice would corrupt the census for the rest of the
// block.
export function stepCensusBackwardOverOp(row BitWordArray, buf OperandList, liveCount RegCount, census RegPoolCensus, effPoolSizes RegCountColumn, fullPoolSize RegCount, ids ValueIdArray, wasLive BoolArray) returns RegCount
	ids.clear()
	wasLive.clear()
	for o in buf 'snapshot'
		if not operandIsVirtual(o) 'physical'
			continue
		end 'physical'
		let v = operandValueId(o)
		if valueIdListContains(ids, valueId: v) 'alreadySnapshot'
			continue
		end 'alreadySnapshot'
		ids.push(v)
		wasLive.push(bitsetTest(row, base: 0, bit: v))
	end 'snapshot'

	let liveBefore = stepRowBackwardOverOperands(row, base: 0, buf: buf, liveCount: liveCount)

	for k in 0 upto ids.count() 'applyDelta'
		let v = try ids.get(k) otherwise panic("stepCensusBackwardOverOp: ids.get OOB at {k}")
		let was = try wasLive.get(k) otherwise panic("stepCensusBackwardOverOp: wasLive.get OOB at {k}")
		let now = bitsetTest(row, base: 0, bit: v)
		if was == now 'unchanged'
			continue
		end 'unchanged'

		let size = try effPoolSizes.get(v) otherwise panic("stepCensusBackwardOverOp: value {v} is outside the function's value space")
		if now 'entered'
			census.add(size, fullPoolSize: fullPoolSize)
		end 'entered' else 'left'
			census.remove(size, fullPoolSize: fullPoolSize)
		end 'left'
	end 'applyDelta'

	return liveBefore
end 'stepCensusBackwardOverOp'

// Is `valueId` already in `ids`? A linear scan, and deliberately so: `ids` holds one op's distinct
// virtual operands — at most a handful — so a set would cost more to build than the scan costs to
// run.
function valueIdListContains(ids ValueIdArray, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	for existing in ids 'each'
		if existing == valueId 'match'
			return true
		end 'match'
	end 'each'
	return false
end 'valueIdListContains'

// ==========================================================================
// Stage 2 — the exact confirmation (maximum bipartite matching)
// ==========================================================================

// The exact confirmation's five working columns, allocated ONCE and reused at every point it runs at.
//
// They used to be allocated PER CALL — and `parentOfReg` + `queue` PER VALUE, inside `augmentValue` —
// so one verdict over `L` live values cost `2L + 4` heap arrays. See the file header for why the call
// count is the problem: a confined value is confined at every op it is live at, so on the shape that
// spills (accumulators across a call in a loop) this runs at EVERY op of the loop, on EVERY splitter
// iteration.
//
// Reusing them changes NO arithmetic: every column is fully re-initialized over the extent this
// point's live list actually reads (`hallVerdictAt` refills both matching maps, `augmentValue` refills
// its parent links, and both walks `clear()` the shared queue), so the augmenting searches run in the
// same order, over the same edges, and land on the same matching. `queue` is shared by the two graph
// walks because they never overlap — `tightRegisterSet` runs only after the last `augmentValue`
// returns.
//
// `regOfValue` HOLDS REGISTER NUMBERS AND IS FINALLY TYPED AS ONE, and the story of why it was not is
// worth keeping. `RegNum` is `int(0 to 16)`, a NARROW range, and a narrow range is a narrow ELEMENT:
// `sizeof(int(0 to 16)) == 1` against `sizeof(int(0 to u64.max)) == 8`. This column used to be refilled
// through `refillColumn`, a FREE function whose parameter was a `DenseColumn` — an 8-byte element — and
// the type checker of the day accepted the substitution silently, because a `typealias` over a narrower
// range unified with the wider one. Declaring this field `RegNumColumn` therefore handed a
// 1-byte-per-element array to code that addressed it 8 bytes per element, and the result was memory
// corruption whose SYMPTOM was a false `E5001` on a program that fits the machine — and whose failure
// set MOVED when unrelated allocations elsewhere changed the heap layout. It cost a day. So the field
// was widened to `DenseColumn` and a comment told you not to fix the name.
//
// BOTH halves of that are now gone. `e4146cf8e` made a generic's ranged element type part of its type,
// so the silent substitution is a compile error (E3005) rather than corruption; and the refill is now
// `Array.refill`, a METHOD, which is generic over the element type and cannot be handed the wrong
// width at all. Every value that goes in is a `RegNum` — a register `0..15`, or the `noRegister`
// sentinel, which is `RegisterFileSize` (16) and exactly why `RegNum`'s bound is 16 and not 15.
export type HallScratch
	// value position -> the registers it is allowed (`A(v)`), for THIS point's live list.
	export var allowed as RegMaskColumn
	// The matching, as its two inverse maps (positions in `live`, not ValueIds).
	export var regOfValue as RegNumColumn
	export var valueOfReg as LiveIndexColumn
	// One augmenting search's parent links: register -> the value position that offered it.
	export var parentOfReg as LiveIndexColumn
	// The worklist both alternating-path walks drive off.
	export var queue as LiveIndexColumn

	export static function create() returns HallScratch
		return Self{allowed: RegMaskColumn.create(), regOfValue: RegNumColumn.create(), valueOfReg: LiveIndexColumn.create(), parentOfReg: LiveIndexColumn.create(), queue: LiveIndexColumn.create()}
	end 'create'
end 'HallScratch'

// What the exact test says about one program point.
export union HallVerdict
	// Every live value can be given a DISTINCT register it is allowed to take. The point colours,
	// whatever the screen suspected.
	feasible
	// It cannot. `witness` is the register set the overflow is against (the callee-saved subset at a
	// call, `pool ∖ {rax,rdx}` at an `idiv`, or — the case this file was written for — the
	// callee-saved subset at a point that is not a call at all). `tight` is how many live values are
	// confined to it, so `tight - popcount(witness)` is the EXACT number that must leave.
	overflow(witness RegMask, tight RegCount)
end 'HallVerdict'

// The EXACT verdict for one program point: can the values in `live` be given distinct registers they
// are each allowed to take?
//
// This is a maximum bipartite matching — values on one side, the 16 physical registers on the other,
// an edge wherever `A(v)` permits. Hall's theorem: the point colours iff the matching SATURATES the
// values. The DEFICIT is `|live| - |matching|` (König–Ore deficiency), and the witness falls out of
// the same alternating-path search that proved the matching maximum.
//
// It runs only where the cardinality screen already fired AND the point does not overflow the full
// pool, so `live.count() <= poolSize <= RegisterFileSize`: the search is bounded by 16 values × 16
// registers and cannot be a hot-path cost.
//
// It answers FEASIBILITY, not the colorer's CHOICE. The colorer is a dominance-order greedy, exact
// for this constraint family because it prefers caller-saved registers first (so an unconstrained
// value never takes a callee-saved one while a caller-saved one is free) and because a copy hint may
// never take a register outside a value's preferred VOLATILITY while that half still has one free
// (`preferredVolatilityMask`, M5.12). A point this call declares feasible is one the colorer can
// colour. (Both are read within the value's own register FILE — a class-agnostic pool question is
// exactly the confusion the volatility rename exists to end.)
export function hallVerdictAt(liveness LivenessResult, live ValueIdArray, pool RegMask, scratch HallScratch) returns HallVerdict
	let valueCount = live.count()
	// "No register" / "no value" markers, each one past the last valid index of its side.
	let noRegister = RegisterFileSize as RegNum
	let noValue = valueCount

	var allowed = scratch.allowed
	allowed.resize(valueCount)
	for i in 0 upto valueCount 'eachValue'
		let v = try live.get(i) otherwise panic("hallVerdictAt: live.get OOB at {i}")
		try allowed.set(i, value: allowedRegistersOf(liveness, valueId: v, pool: pool)) otherwise panic("hallVerdictAt: allowed.set OOB at {i}")
	end 'eachValue'

	// The matching, as its two inverse maps. Positions in `live`, not ValueIds — the search is over
	// this point's live set alone, so a dense 0..valueCount numbering is the right one. `noValue` IS
	// `valueCount`, so `valueOfReg` must be re-filled on every call, not merely on a resize.
	var regOfValue = scratch.regOfValue
	regOfValue.refill(valueCount, value: noRegister)
	var valueOfReg = scratch.valueOfReg
	valueOfReg.refill(RegisterFileSize, value: noValue)

	var matched = 0 as RegCount
	for i in 0 upto valueCount 'augment'
		if augmentValue(i, scratch: scratch, noRegister: noRegister, noValue: noValue) 'grew'
			matched = matched + 1
		end 'grew'
	end 'augment'

	if matched == valueCount 'saturated'
		return HallVerdict.feasible
	end 'saturated'

	let witness = tightRegisterSet(scratch, valueCount: valueCount, noRegister: noRegister, noValue: noValue)
	let tight = countConfinedTo(allowed, witness: witness, valueCount: valueCount)

	// The tight set is exactly the unmatched values plus the holders of the witness registers — every
	// witness register is held (an unheld one would have been an augmenting path), and every value
	// confined to the witness is reachable through the register it holds. So the two ways of counting
	// it must agree, and a disagreement means the matching or the reachability walk is wrong — which
	// would silently corrupt the deficit this reports.
	let expected = regMaskPopcount(witness) + (valueCount - matched)
	if tight != expected 'inconsistentTightSet'
		panic("hallVerdictAt: {tight} live values are confined to the witness {witness} but the matching's tight set has {expected} (witness {regMaskPopcount(witness)} + unmatched {valueCount - matched}) — the maximum matching and its alternating-path reachability disagree")
	end 'inconsistentTightSet'

	return HallVerdict.overflow(witness, tight: tight)
end 'hallVerdictAt'

// One augmenting search from value position `start`, growing the matching by one if it can.
//
// Alternating paths: `start` offers a register it is allowed; if the register is FREE, take it; if it
// is HELD, the holder is queued and must find another register for itself, freeing this one. Reaching
// a free register means the whole chain can shift by one and `start` ends up matched.
//
// ITERATIVE — a worklist plus one parent link per register — like every other graph walk in the
// allocator. The parent links are not just an anti-recursion device: they ARE the path the
// augmentation walks back along.
function augmentValue(start LiveIndex, scratch HallScratch, noRegister RegNum, noValue LiveIndex) returns bool
	var allowed = scratch.allowed
	var valueOfReg = scratch.valueOfReg
	// register -> the value position that offered it during THIS search (its predecessor on the
	// alternating path). Only registers in `visited` have a meaningful entry, and `visited` is local to
	// this search — but the column is re-filled anyway, so a stale link can never be read even if that
	// reasoning is ever broken by an edit.
	var parentOfReg = scratch.parentOfReg
	parentOfReg.refill(RegisterFileSize, value: noValue)
	var visited = 0 as RegMask
	var queue = scratch.queue
	queue.clear()
	queue.push(start)

	var head = 0 as ColumnLength
	while head < queue.count() 'alternatingPaths'
		let u = try queue.get(head) otherwise panic("augmentValue: queue.get OOB at {head}")
		head = head + 1

		let mask = try allowed.get(u) otherwise panic("augmentValue: allowed.get OOB for value position {u}")
		for r in 0 upto RegisterFileSize 'eachRegister'
			if not regMaskContains(mask, regNum: r) 'notAllowed'
				continue
			end 'notAllowed'
			if regMaskContains(visited, regNum: r) 'alreadyOnAPath'
				continue
			end 'alreadyOnAPath'

			visited = regMaskAdd(visited, regNum: r)
			try parentOfReg.set(r, value: u) otherwise panic("augmentValue: parentOfReg.set OOB at {r}")

			let holder = try valueOfReg.get(r) otherwise panic("augmentValue: valueOfReg.get OOB at {r}")
			if holder == noValue 'freeRegister'
				applyAugmentingPath(r, start: start, scratch: scratch, noRegister: noRegister)
				return true
			end 'freeRegister'

			queue.push(holder)
		end 'eachRegister'
	end 'alternatingPaths'

	return false
end 'augmentValue'

// Flip every edge on the alternating path from the free register `freeReg` back to `start`: each
// register on it is re-assigned to the value that offered it, and THAT value's previous register —
// vacated by the re-assignment — is the next one to hand on. The walk ends at `start`, which held no
// register, so the matching finishes one edge larger.
function applyAugmentingPath(freeReg RegNum, start LiveIndex, scratch HallScratch, noRegister RegNum)
	var parentOfReg = scratch.parentOfReg
	var regOfValue = scratch.regOfValue
	var valueOfReg = scratch.valueOfReg
	var reg = freeReg
	var reachedStart = false
	while not reachedStart 'walkBack'
		let u = try parentOfReg.get(reg) otherwise panic("applyAugmentingPath: parentOfReg.get OOB at {reg}")
		let vacated = try regOfValue.get(u) otherwise panic("applyAugmentingPath: regOfValue.get OOB for value position {u}")

		try valueOfReg.set(reg, value: u) otherwise panic("applyAugmentingPath: valueOfReg.set OOB at {reg}")
		try regOfValue.set(u, value: reg) otherwise panic("applyAugmentingPath: regOfValue.set OOB for value position {u}")

		reachedStart = u == start
		if (not reachedStart) and vacated == noRegister 'brokenPath'
			panic("applyAugmentingPath: value position {u} is on the alternating path but held no register, yet it is not the search's start — only the start is unmatched, so the parent links are corrupt")
		end 'brokenPath'
		reg = vacated
	end 'walkBack'
end 'applyAugmentingPath'

// The Hall WITNESS: the registers reachable from the UNMATCHED values along alternating paths.
//
// Every one of them is held — an unheld reachable register would have been an augmenting path, and
// the matching is already maximum, so the walk asserts that rather than assume it. The values that
// reach them (the unmatched ones, plus each holder) are therefore confined to exactly this register
// set and outnumber it by the deficiency. That is the set Hall's condition fails on, and it is why
// removing any ONE of its values drops the deficiency by exactly one: the freed register lets an
// unmatched value augment into the chain.
function tightRegisterSet(scratch HallScratch, valueCount LiveIndex, noRegister RegNum, noValue LiveIndex) returns RegMask
	var allowed = scratch.allowed
	var regOfValue = scratch.regOfValue
	var valueOfReg = scratch.valueOfReg
	var reachable = 0 as RegMask
	// The queue is shared with `augmentValue`, which has finished by the time this runs (the matching
	// must be MAXIMUM before its tight set means anything).
	var queue = scratch.queue
	queue.clear()
	for i in 0 upto valueCount 'seedUnmatched'
		if (try regOfValue.get(i) otherwise panic("tightRegisterSet: regOfValue.get OOB at {i}")) == noRegister 'unmatched'
			queue.push(i)
		end 'unmatched'
	end 'seedUnmatched'

	var head = 0 as ColumnLength
	while head < queue.count() 'alternatingPaths'
		let u = try queue.get(head) otherwise panic("tightRegisterSet: queue.get OOB at {head}")
		head = head + 1

		let mask = try allowed.get(u) otherwise panic("tightRegisterSet: allowed.get OOB for value position {u}")
		for r in 0 upto RegisterFileSize 'eachRegister'
			if not regMaskContains(mask, regNum: r) 'notAllowed'
				continue
			end 'notAllowed'
			if regMaskContains(reachable, regNum: r) 'alreadyReached'
				continue
			end 'alreadyReached'

			reachable = regMaskAdd(reachable, regNum: r)

			let holder = try valueOfReg.get(r) otherwise panic("tightRegisterSet: valueOfReg.get OOB at {r}")
			if holder == noValue 'freeRegister'
				panic("tightRegisterSet: register {r} is reachable from an unmatched value along an alternating path yet is held by nobody — that is an augmenting path, so the matching `augmentValue` produced is not maximum")
			end 'freeRegister'

			queue.push(holder)
		end 'eachRegister'
	end 'alternatingPaths'

	return reachable
end 'tightRegisterSet'

// How many of the point's live values are confined to `witness` — every register they could take
// lies inside it. This is the set the overflow is about, and the number the deficit is measured from.
function countConfinedTo(allowed RegMaskColumn, witness RegMask, valueCount LiveIndex) returns RegCount
	var confined = 0 as RegCount
	for i in 0 upto valueCount 'eachValue'
		let mask = try allowed.get(i) otherwise panic("countConfinedTo: allowed.get OOB at {i}")
		if (mask and not witness) == 0 'insideWitness'
			confined = confined + 1
		end 'insideWitness'
	end 'eachValue'
	return confined
end 'countConfinedTo'
